# Copy to `.env` and edit.
#
# This file feeds TWO processes, and the prefix is what separates them:
#
#   VITE_*   reaches the browser. Vite inlines these into the bundle at BUILD
#            time, so anyone with the app can read them, and a production build
#            carries whatever was set when it was built — rebuild after a change.
#
#   no prefix   stays on the server (`server/config.ts`). Vite's default
#               `envPrefix` is `['VITE_']`, so it drops these on the floor and
#               they never reach a phone.
#
# That split is load-bearing: UPSTREAM_URL below names the real government
# service, and it must never be inlined into JavaScript a phone downloads. See
# "The DTO boundary" in the README.

# ── Servidor (NUNCA prefixe com VITE_) ───────────────────────────────────────

# Answer from server/shared/mock-upstream.ts instead of calling Mais Cidadão.
#
# DEFAULTS TO TRUE, and leaving it that way is the safe choice. Permission to
# call the real service belongs to a place and a person — not to whoever has
# this repository checked out. On any machine that is not the authorised one,
# this stays on and NO request ever leaves the machine.
#
# Set to false ONLY on the deployment machine, and only once. Every protocol it
# then files is definitive; there is no undo.
#
# Mocked, the server still returns a full upstream-shaped payload — fake CPF and
# address included — so the DTOs and the selection logic run for real. See
# "The DTO boundary" in the README.
MOCK_UPSTREAM=true

# The Mais Cidadão service. Scheme and host, no trailing slash, no path.
#
# Only read when MOCK_UPSTREAM=false — and then it is REQUIRED: the server
# refuses to boot without it. There is no safe default. A guessed URL either
# fails everything or silently points a batch of real submissions somewhere
# nobody intended.
# UPSTREAM_URL=https://exemplo-do-servico.gov.br

# The port serving the app AND the API, together, from one process. 4173
# because phones already have it bookmarked from when `vite preview` lived here.
PORT=4173

# How long to wait on the Mais Cidadão service, in milliseconds. Keep this
# BELOW VITE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS: the server should always be the one to give up
# first, so the phone gets a definite 504 rather than timing out on its own and
# never learning whether the submission landed.
UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# There is no HTTPS setting here. Use `pnpm server:http` when you want plain
# HTTP, which is useful for exactly one thing: http://localhost:4173 counts as a
# secure context, so the camera and the service worker both work there with no
# certificate at all. Never for a phone — over a LAN IP, plain HTTP blocks
# getUserMedia outright.
#
# A flag rather than an env var because `HTTPS=false cmd` is not something
# Windows' cmd or PowerShell understands, and Windows is the deployment target.

# ── App ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# When true (the default), the Vite dev server answers every API route itself,
# from plugins/mock-api.ts. Set to false to talk to the Express server in
# server/ — which talks to the real service, and files real protocols.
#
# Leaving it unset means mocked, on purpose: an accidental real submission to a
# government service is much worse than an accidental mock.
VITE_USE_MOCK=true

# Per-request timeout in milliseconds, browser side. It now covers the whole
# chain — phone → Express → Mais Cidadão — so it is deliberately generous, and
# deliberately larger than UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS above.
VITE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=25000

# There is no VITE_API_URL and no VITE_AUTH_TOKEN any more.
#
#   The URL is gone because every call is now a same-origin relative path
#   (`/api/...`) answered by the server in server/. Nothing to configure, and it
#   works on any network with no per-machine setup.
#
#   The token is gone because it was baked into the bundle, identical for every
#   user, and readable by anyone with the app. It has been replaced by a login:
#   see server/auth/ and src/lib/session.ts.
